WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.647
This is the executive briefing
on external partner identity,

3
00:00:04.647 --> 00:00:09.218
from the HR-Driven Identity book of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

4
00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:12.799
with its partner access profile
in Access and Privilege.

5
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:17.585
It's for the people who make the calls:
the CIO and CISO, the business owners of

6
00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:22.750
programs shared with partners, the privacy
officer and the authorizing official.

7
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.625
Many people who sign in to agency
systems work for someone else.

8
00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:30.225
The workbook now treats them
as their own identity class.

9
00:00:30.225 --> 00:00:33.355
I'll take four questions:
why the class exists,

10
00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:36.738
what it is, who decides,
and how a partner goes live.

11
00:00:36.738 --> 00:00:39.590
The last slide gathers the decisions.

12
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.581
Every earlier chapter of HR-Driven
Identity rests on one rule:

13
00:00:47.581 --> 00:00:52.902
the agency's own authoritative source
says who works here, and access follows.

14
00:00:52.902 --> 00:00:57.991
Employees come from HR; contractors
from the source the agency designates.

15
00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:01.971
But think of a state benefits
examiner, a tribal health officer,

16
00:01:01.971 --> 00:01:06.565
an analyst at another agency, or a
compliance officer at a regulated bank.

17
00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:10.390
Each of them is employed, proofed
and credentialed by someone else.

18
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.959
When that state examiner
leaves the state government,

19
00:01:13.959 --> 00:01:17.290
the state's HR system knows
first, and possibly only.

20
00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.894
The agency can't key, correct
or even see that record,

21
00:01:20.894 --> 00:01:23.640
except through what the partner sends.

22
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.828
So a partner's account proves that
another organization vouches for a person.

23
00:01:27.828 --> 00:01:31.950
It doesn't prove that the agency
has decided to let that person in.

24
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.863
The split of authority is
the definition of the class.

25
00:01:38.863 --> 00:01:42.189
The partner is authoritative
for the person's affiliation,

26
00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:46.737
who works there and in what role, and for
the lifecycle of the credential it issues.

27
00:01:46.737 --> 00:01:50.898
The agency is authoritative for
everything that touches its systems:

28
00:01:50.898 --> 00:01:54.198
whether it trusts the partner's
identity service at all,

29
00:01:54.198 --> 00:01:57.497
which partner persons are
eligible, what they may reach,

30
00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:02.088
what assurance each transaction needs,
and when access is suspended or ended.

31
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.451
One rule follows.

32
00:02:03.451 --> 00:02:07.888
No partner account gets access just
because it exists or synchronizes.

33
00:02:07.888 --> 00:02:11.525
An account in the tenant is a fact
about the partner's directory.

34
00:02:11.525 --> 00:02:18.120
Access is an agency decision, with an
approver, a sponsor, an expiry and evidence.

35
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:27.075
The External Partner Person is a person
whose employment or affiliation record,

36
00:02:27.075 --> 00:02:31.271
and primary credential, are held
by another organization: a state,

37
00:02:31.271 --> 00:02:34.146
a tribal government,
another federal agency,

38
00:02:34.146 --> 00:02:38.108
a financial institution, or an
academic or nonprofit partner.

39
00:02:38.108 --> 00:02:41.771
The class covers people; the
organization is the partner.

40
00:02:41.771 --> 00:02:44.872
The workbook's other
populations already have homes,

41
00:02:44.872 --> 00:02:48.257
so the chapter draws borders
rather than redefining them.

42
00:02:48.257 --> 00:02:52.500
Partner is not public: a member of
the public applying for a benefit

43
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.433
isn't a workforce guest, even if
a partner delivers the benefit.

44
00:02:56.433 --> 00:03:01.384
Partner is not contractor: if the agency
holds the contract and the sponsor,

45
00:03:01.384 --> 00:03:05.857
and the person works on agency
business, that person is a contractor.

46
00:03:05.857 --> 00:03:08.486
Privilege and break-glass are overlays,

47
00:03:08.486 --> 00:03:12.308
and emergency access accounts
are always the agency's own.

48
00:03:12.308 --> 00:03:18.140
And an AI agent acting for a partner is a
non-person identity with its own sponsor.

49
00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.887
The workbook uses three
words for populations,

50
00:03:24.887 --> 00:03:27.115
and each is a view of the one above it.

51
00:03:27.115 --> 00:03:30.490
The class says who is
authoritative and who owns access.

52
00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.369
Worker type is the HR attribute
for the agency's own workers,

53
00:03:34.369 --> 00:03:38.565
and a partner person has none,
because there's no agency HR record.

54
00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:42.827
The persona is how Conditional Access
targets people, and for partners

55
00:03:42.827 --> 00:03:46.727
that's the Externals persona, which
must match the class one to one.

56
00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:50.202
The user type is where those
views can quietly diverge.

57
00:03:50.202 --> 00:03:54.512
The workbook's member baselines, such
as the compliant-device policies,

58
00:03:54.512 --> 00:03:58.018
exclude guests, so a partner
created as a member would fall

59
00:03:58.018 --> 00:04:01.378
into the internal baselines
and out of the partner ones.

60
00:04:01.378 --> 00:04:03.828
So partner persons are guests by default.

61
00:04:03.828 --> 00:04:07.059
A member partner person
needs an exception record,

62
00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:10.120
with a reason, an approver
and a review date.

63
00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.870
The standard is short on purpose.

64
00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:20.978
It says what has to be true for any
partner, on any platform, in nine areas:

65
00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:26.438
partner qualification, trust establishment,
population scope, assurance,

66
00:04:26.438 --> 00:04:31.458
authorization, lifecycle, data
protection, operations and evidence.

67
00:04:31.458 --> 00:04:35.044
Each area becomes a section of
the partner identity record.

68
00:04:35.044 --> 00:04:37.420
Three points matter most to leadership.

69
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:42.472
Authorization is separate from authentication:
a valid sign-in at the state proves

70
00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:47.158
the examiner signed in there; only an
agency assignment opens the case system.

71
00:04:47.158 --> 00:04:51.135
Partner exit is a lifecycle
event: when an agreement ends,

72
00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:54.182
the accounts are removed,
not left disabled.

73
00:04:54.182 --> 00:04:59.366
And statements are not controls: a
partner saying it requires MFA is input

74
00:04:59.366 --> 00:05:05.070
to the agreement, while a policy the
agency enforces and tests is the control.

75
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:09.845
Who decides?

76
00:05:09.845 --> 00:05:13.831
The partner and the agency each
carry part of every activity,

77
00:05:13.831 --> 00:05:18.933
from affiliation and proofing to
lifecycle, incident response and evidence.

78
00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:23.355
On the agency side there is one decider
per row: the agency business owner,

79
00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:27.557
the official accountable for the
program the partner persons serve.

80
00:05:27.557 --> 00:05:32.095
The title is provisional, and the
record names a person, not a title.

81
00:05:32.095 --> 00:05:36.171
The sponsor, usually the program
manager who works with the partner,

82
00:05:36.171 --> 00:05:40.466
vouches for its people, reviews their
access and requests suspensions,

83
00:05:40.466 --> 00:05:42.795
but never approves their own requests.

84
00:05:42.795 --> 00:05:47.520
The identity governance officer owns the
list of approved partner organizations.

85
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.058
Two rows need comment.

86
00:05:49.058 --> 00:05:52.106
In a live incident, security
operations contains

87
00:05:52.106 --> 00:05:54.708
first and the business
owner is told after.

88
00:05:54.708 --> 00:05:58.544
And going to production has one
decider with a required consult:

89
00:05:58.544 --> 00:06:01.583
the authorizing official's
input must be on record.

90
00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:06.223
A consult isn't a second approval, but an
objection the business owner overrides

91
00:06:06.223 --> 00:06:10.940
becomes a risk acceptance, and that is
the authorizing official's decision.

92
00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:21.837
NIST SP 800-63-4 measures
digital identity on three scales:

93
00:06:21.837 --> 00:06:25.178
identity proofing,
authentication, and federation.

94
00:06:25.178 --> 00:06:28.974
For a partner person, the partner
usually performs the first two,

95
00:06:28.974 --> 00:06:32.914
and the agency decides whether
that's enough for each transaction.

96
00:06:32.914 --> 00:06:35.287
The levels come from an impact assessment,

97
00:06:35.287 --> 00:06:38.065
and partner persons are
their own user group in it.

98
00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:42.837
A bank officer submitting a financial
filing and a state examiner reading a case

99
00:06:42.837 --> 00:06:46.114
summary can land at different
levels on the same system,

100
00:06:46.114 --> 00:06:50.815
so the record holds one row per protected
transaction, not one level per partner.

101
00:06:50.815 --> 00:06:52.627
Two federal rules shape this.

102
00:06:52.627 --> 00:06:59.061
M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant MFA
for agency staff, contractors and partners,

103
00:06:59.061 --> 00:07:02.681
but leaves each agency to
define the partner category,

104
00:07:02.681 --> 00:07:06.139
so the agency writes its
definition into the record.

105
00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:11.435
And FIPS 201-3 covers federal employees
and contractors only, so never call a

106
00:07:11.435 --> 00:07:16.570
partner credential PIV-equivalent unless
a policy the agency can cite says so.

107
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.258
The route into the tenant is the
same in principle on any platform.

108
00:07:24.258 --> 00:07:28.899
First, the partner is qualified: the
record exists, the agreement is signed,

109
00:07:28.899 --> 00:07:31.790
and the partner's identity
service is trusted

110
00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.682
only through a setting
scoped to that partner.

111
00:07:34.682 --> 00:07:37.806
Second, each person
arrives through an approved

112
00:07:37.806 --> 00:07:41.320
request naming the sponsor,
the approver and an expiry.

113
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.545
Third, the account is a guest.

114
00:07:43.545 --> 00:07:46.396
Fourth, access ends with
the last assignment,

115
00:07:46.396 --> 00:07:49.170
and the account is
blocked and then removed.

116
00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:53.093
Automatic provisioning from a
partner's directory is an exception,

117
00:07:53.093 --> 00:07:57.577
because it hands the eligibility decision
to whoever controls the partner's

118
00:07:57.577 --> 00:08:01.570
groups and copies personal data
across organizations on a schedule.

119
00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.694
In Microsoft Entra, the
default is entitlement

120
00:08:04.694 --> 00:08:07.658
management with B2B collaboration guests.

121
00:08:07.658 --> 00:08:12.399
Microsoft Learn says cross-tenant
synchronization is intended for use within an

122
00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:14.770
organization, and points to entitlement

123
00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.270
management for inviting
users across organizations.

124
00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.220
Four obligations come before
the first partner goes live.

125
00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.693
Privacy: a directory of partner persons,

126
00:08:27.693 --> 00:08:32.788
their access decisions and their sign-in
logs is retrieved by name and identifier,

127
00:08:32.788 --> 00:08:36.385
so whether an existing
system-of-records notice covers it

128
00:08:36.385 --> 00:08:39.982
is the privacy office's
determination, made before go-live.

129
00:08:39.982 --> 00:08:45.248
Minimization: the agency receives only
the attributes its decisions read,

130
00:08:45.248 --> 00:08:48.900
usually a name, a work email,
an immutable identifier,

131
00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:52.382
the partner organization
and, where needed, a role.

132
00:08:52.382 --> 00:08:57.074
Records: NARA's General Records Schedule
3.2 covers system access records,

133
00:08:57.074 --> 00:09:02.381
but the schedules for partner agreements
and access reviews haven't been verified,

134
00:09:02.381 --> 00:09:04.996
so the agency records officer names them.

135
00:09:04.996 --> 00:09:09.940
And redress: a wrong name is corrected
at the partner's source, while an agency

136
00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:14.807
decision, such as a suspension, is
disputed through the agency's own process.

137
00:09:14.807 --> 00:09:20.280
The request and redress workflows
are covered by Section 508, too.

138
00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:28.618
Eleven readiness checks stand between
a qualified partner and production.

139
00:09:28.618 --> 00:09:33.118
They aren't the program's phase gates;
they're specific to each relationship.

140
00:09:33.118 --> 00:09:37.317
They confirm the class and the
partner's authoritative source;

141
00:09:37.317 --> 00:09:41.599
record which requirements apply;
review the agreement, privacy,

142
00:09:41.599 --> 00:09:46.688
records and the authorization boundary;
select assurance per transaction;

143
00:09:46.688 --> 00:09:49.757
name owners and test
the escalation contacts;

144
00:09:49.757 --> 00:09:55.331
reconcile the expected population against
the accounts; minimize the attributes;

145
00:09:55.331 --> 00:10:00.177
test create, update, disable, removal,
emergency suspension and rollback;

146
00:10:00.177 --> 00:10:04.216
test access, including denials;
and confirm that monitoring,

147
00:10:04.216 --> 00:10:07.518
reviews, drift detection
and runbooks are operating.

148
00:10:07.518 --> 00:10:10.505
The eleventh is production
enablement itself.

149
00:10:10.505 --> 00:10:13.479
Each check has one decider,
the business owner,

150
00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:15.905
and the checks repeat at every review.

151
00:10:15.905 --> 00:10:19.700
A partner that adds a new
application reruns four of them.

152
00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.450
Here are the decisions, with
their owners and timing.

153
00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.467
Before the first partner goes live,

154
00:10:28.467 --> 00:10:32.920
the privacy office determines whether
an existing system-of-records notice

155
00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.650
covers partner persons, and the records
officer names the records schedule items.

156
00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.100
For each partner, the business
owner decides five things.

157
00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:46.350
Qualifying the partner and approving the
agreement, before any trust is configured.

158
00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.019
Which requirements apply,

159
00:10:48.019 --> 00:10:52.800
including the agency's own definition
of partners under M-22-09.

160
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.954
The assurance for each
protected transaction,

161
00:10:55.954 --> 00:10:59.350
with the ISSO and the
application owner consulted.

162
00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.240
Any automatic synchronization,

163
00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:04.950
which is an exception on a written
record, with the sponsor named.

164
00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:09.450
And production enablement, with the
authorizing official's consult on record.

165
00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.054
A member user type is an exception too,

166
00:11:12.054 --> 00:11:15.163
with its own record,
approver and review date.

167
00:11:15.163 --> 00:11:18.234
The chapter, the partner
identity record and the

168
00:11:18.234 --> 00:11:21.688
Entra profile carry the
detail, every claim footnoted.

169
00:11:21.688 --> 00:11:22.510
Thank you.
